1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,885
In this video we'll set up the AIFS prototype — ckptfs

2
00:00:03,885 --> 00:00:08,190
— from a clean Ubuntu machine to a working, accelerated checkpoint mount.

3
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:12,201
By the end you'll have built the filesystem from source, mounted it,

4
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:16,979
written a real checkpoint through it, and watched it replicate to durable storage

5
00:00:16,979 --> 00:00:19,574
— plus what to do when something goes wrong.

6
00:00:23,699 --> 00:00:26,465
Before installing anything, the mental model.

7
00:00:26,465 --> 00:00:28,370
ckptfs takes three directories.

8
00:00:28,370 --> 00:00:32,180
The mountpoint is the virtual filesystem your applications see

9
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:34,331
— nothing is actually stored there.

10
00:00:34,331 --> 00:00:38,325
The spool is fast local NVMe — every new write lands there first.

11
00:00:38,325 --> 00:00:42,996
And the lower directory is your durable storage — an NFS share in production

12
00:00:42,996 --> 00:00:46,069
— which remains the source of truth after a crash.

13
00:00:46,069 --> 00:00:49,141
One binary, three paths — that's the whole system.

14
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:59,138
You'll need a Linux machine — Ubuntu 22.04 or later, or WSL2 on Windows

15
00:00:59,138 --> 00:01:00,594
— with sudo access.

16
00:01:00,594 --> 00:01:06,262
The toolchain is four packages: build-essential for the C compiler, fuse3,

17
00:01:06,262 --> 00:01:10,016
the libfuse3 development headers, and pkg-config.

18
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,762
Start with apt-get update, then install the four packages.

19
00:01:16,762 --> 00:01:19,984
On a fresh machine this takes a minute or two.

20
00:01:19,984 --> 00:01:25,798
When it finishes, verify GCC is available — version thirteen here, on Ubuntu 24.04.

21
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:33,844
Clone the repository from GitHub, change into it, and run make.

22
00:01:33,844 --> 00:01:38,878
The build takes a few seconds and produces a single small binary: ckptfs.

23
00:01:42,099 --> 00:01:47,387
Create the three directories — the mountpoint, the durable backend, and the NVMe spool

24
00:01:47,387 --> 00:01:49,232
— then take ownership of them.

25
00:01:49,232 --> 00:01:52,306
Launch ckptfs with the three paths, in that order.

26
00:01:52,306 --> 00:01:57,655
It runs in the foreground, so keep this terminal open and do your work in a second one.

27
00:02:00,399 --> 00:02:03,239
From a second terminal, confirm the mount is live.

28
00:02:03,239 --> 00:02:05,567
Then write a test file through it with dd

29
00:02:05,567 --> 00:02:10,508
— note the gigabyte-per-second speed: that write landed on the local spool immediately.

30
00:02:10,508 --> 00:02:13,915
Moments later, the same file appears in the durable backend,

31
00:02:13,915 --> 00:02:16,755
together with a hidden marker: dot aifs committed.

32
00:02:16,755 --> 00:02:20,730
That marker means the whole checkpoint directory is safely replicated.

33
00:02:23,899 --> 00:02:25,252
Now the real workflow.

34
00:02:25,252 --> 00:02:30,233
We save a multi-file training checkpoint — model, optimizer, scheduler, RNG state

35
00:02:30,233 --> 00:02:34,107
— while refreshing both directory listings five times a second.

36
00:02:34,107 --> 00:02:39,149
The spool on the left fills instantly — a training job would already be unblocked.

37
00:02:39,149 --> 00:02:43,514
On the right, the background replicator copies each file over in order,

38
00:02:43,514 --> 00:02:47,142
and the commit marker arrives last, sealing the checkpoint.

39
00:02:47,142 --> 00:02:49,417
On real hardware with an NFS backend,

40
00:02:49,417 --> 00:02:54,951
that right-hand column trails by thirty to forty seconds — and the GPUs never wait for it.

41
00:03:03,899 --> 00:03:05,067
What about crashes?

42
00:03:05,067 --> 00:03:07,462
Kill ckptfs mid-flight, and restart it.

43
00:03:07,462 --> 00:03:11,823
On startup, the old spool is quarantined — never trusted after a crash.

44
00:03:11,823 --> 00:03:15,447
The durable backend still holds every committed checkpoint.

45
00:03:15,447 --> 00:03:20,852
The recovery rule is simple: resume training from the newest checkpoint that carries the

46
00:03:20,852 --> 00:03:21,712
commit marker.

47
00:03:24,899 --> 00:03:28,783
This demo ran in a container, so both tiers shared one disk.

48
00:03:28,783 --> 00:03:34,284
On a real cloud VM, you first provision the local NVMe disk: partition it with fdisk,

49
00:03:34,284 --> 00:03:38,944
format it e x t 4, and mount it — the full transcript is in the read-me,

50
00:03:38,944 --> 00:03:40,951
recorded on an Azure L8 a s v3.

51
00:03:40,951 --> 00:03:46,388
One warning before you use the bundled setup script: it force-formats the first NVMe

52
00:03:46,388 --> 00:03:49,753
device it finds, and on many VMs that's the OS disk.

53
00:03:49,753 --> 00:03:52,019
Always check the device name first.

54
00:03:54,899 --> 00:03:56,777
Set up this way on real hardware,

55
00:03:56,777 --> 00:04:01,557
the prototype sustained eight hundred seventy-nine megabytes per second on the fully

56
00:04:01,557 --> 00:04:05,939
durable path — a four-gibibyte checkpoint locally safe in under five seconds,

57
00:04:05,939 --> 00:04:10,320
with replication completing off the critical path, about half a minute later.

58
00:04:12,999 --> 00:04:15,107
The issues you're most likely to hit.

59
00:04:15,107 --> 00:04:20,062
If the mount is dead after a crash, unmount with fusermount3 dash u before relaunching.

60
00:04:20,062 --> 00:04:24,903
If ls on the mount shows nothing after a restart, that's a known prototype limitation

61
00:04:24,903 --> 00:04:27,238
— your data is still safe in the backend.

62
00:04:27,238 --> 00:04:31,908
Write checkpoints into their own directories — never loose files at the mount root

63
00:04:31,908 --> 00:04:33,673
— or commit markers won't work.

64
00:04:33,673 --> 00:04:38,344
And clean up quarantined orphan spool directories from time to time; they're never

65
00:04:38,344 --> 00:04:39,597
deleted automatically.

66
00:04:42,499 --> 00:04:47,585
And that's the whole setup: four packages, one make, three directories, one command.

67
00:04:47,585 --> 00:04:51,944
The full install guide and troubleshooting notes live in the repository,

68
00:04:51,944 --> 00:04:53,095
under deliverables.

69
00:04:53,095 --> 00:04:57,878
Try it against your own training pipeline — and stop paying the checkpoint tax.

